// Editor-side companion to this extension.
//
// The extension itself only colors [tag[ ... ]tag] in the Markdown PREVIEW.
// This block colors the same markers in the Markdown SOURCE, which is where
// replies actually get typed. It needs the Highlight extension:
//
//     code --install-extension fabiospampinato.vscode-highlight
//
// Paste into VS Code user settings (Ctrl+Shift+P -> "Preferences: Open User
// Settings (JSON)"), then edit the regex/colors below to match whatever tags
// you configured in `markerHighlight.markers` (see README.md) — this file is
// a static settings snippet, so unlike the preview extension it can't read
// that setting automatically.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// About the convention
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A reviewer answers inline, in the same file, wrapping their reply in
// [tag[ ... ]tag]. Several Claude Code skills depend on this — most notably
// /extensive-feedback, which runs a multi-round file-based Q&A where Claude
// writes numbered questions into a scratch doc and the reviewer answers under
// each one inside these markers. The markers are asymmetric so they stay
// greppable and never collide with real Markdown syntax.
//
// The three `decorations` entries map to the three capture groups: opening
// marker, reply body, closing marker. Markers are dimmed so the eye lands on
// the reply text rather than on the punctuation.
//
// `[\s\S]*?` rather than `.*?` so a reply spanning several lines still matches;
// lazy so two adjacent replies don't merge into one run.
//
// The example below matches the default tag ("comment", orange). Add one
// regex entry per tag you configure, with matching colors.

{
  "highlight.regexFlags": "gi",
  "highlight.regexes": {
    "(\\[comment\\[)([\\s\\S]*?)(\\]comment\\])": {
      "filterLanguageRegex": "markdown",
      "decorations": [
        { "color": "#f0a55899", "fontWeight": "bold" },
        { "color": "#f0a558" },
        { "color": "#f0a55899", "fontWeight": "bold" }
      ]
    }
  }
}
